   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Can Standardized Small-Sided Games Be Used as a Tool to Monitor Post-Match Fatigue in Male Academy Soccer Players?</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Can Standardized Small-Sided Games Be Used as a Tool to Monitor Post-Match Fatigue in Male Academy Soccer Players?</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>athlete monitoring; countermovement jump; neuromuscular fatigue; small-sided games; heart rate variability; youth soccer</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">This study aimed to analyze residual post-match fatigue using time-motion analysis across small-sided games (SSGs)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">This study aimed to analyze residual post-match fatigue using time-motion analysis across small-sided games (SSGs)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">fitness performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">fitness performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and heart rate variability (HRV) in academy soccer players. Fifteen male academy players (18.3 $\pm $ 0.6 years) performed four SSG formats: 3 vs. 3 and 3 vs. 3 + 2 goalkeepers</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and heart rate variability (HRV) in academy soccer players. Fifteen male academy players (18.3 $\pm $ 0.6 years) performed four SSG formats: 3 vs. 3 and 3 vs. 3 + 2 goalkeepers</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">played on small (25 $\times $ 18 m; 75 m2 per player) and large (36 $\times $ 25 m; 150 m2 per player) areas in a four-week experimental design. Two non-match weeks served as baseline weeks</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">played on small (25 $\times $ 18 m; 75 m2 per player) and large (36 $\times $ 25 m; 150 m2 per player) areas in a four-week experimental design. Two non-match weeks served as baseline weeks</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and two weeks followed an official match. Time-motion during SSGs was monitored via GNSS. Before each training session (24</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and two weeks followed an official match. Time-motion during SSGs was monitored via GNSS. Before each training session (24</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">72</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">72</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and 120 h post-match)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and 120 h post-match)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">HRV</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">HRV</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">countermovement jump (CMJ) height</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">countermovement jump (CMJ) height</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and maximum isometric force (MIF) of the hamstrings were measured. External load metrics were generally lower in post-match sessions than in non-match weeks; however</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and maximum isometric force (MIF) of the hamstrings were measured. External load metrics were generally lower in post-match sessions than in non-match weeks; however</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">their low reliability limited the interpretability of SSG outputs. CMJ height was reduced</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">their low reliability limited the interpretability of SSG outputs. CMJ height was reduced</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">MIF was elevated post-match</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">MIF was elevated post-match</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">whereas HRV showed distinct mid-week responses</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">whereas HRV showed distinct mid-week responses</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">decreasing in starters but increasing in non-starters. Post-match fatigue was evident in neuromuscular performance but was inconsistently reflected in SSG outputs. CMJ height reliably reflects post-match fatigue in male academy soccer players and should be prioritized when monitoring neuromuscular recovery.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">decreasing in starters but increasing in non-starters. Post-match fatigue was evident in neuromuscular performance but was inconsistently reflected in SSG outputs. CMJ height reliably reflects post-match fatigue in male academy soccer players and should be prioritized when monitoring neuromuscular recovery.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
